course: dictionary
lesson: 14721
title: Words that claim
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you what a person says about themselves. Profess means to say openly that you feel, believe or have something. «Tom professed his love for cats» means Tom openly told everyone that he loves cats.
explain: Profess can also mean to claim something, and the claim is often not quite true. «Sam professes to know everything, but he spelled 'school' wrong.» The word but is a clue: the claim and the facts do not match. Ask yourself whether the rest of the sentence supports the claim or not.
explain: You will often meet profess in phrases such as «profess to be», «profess ignorance» and «professed love». When you see one, read the words around it. Then swap in a simple meaning, such as say or claim, and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Profess is a formal, bookish verb, so you will mostly meet it in stories and exam passages.
fact: Profess means to say openly that you feel, believe or have something.
fact: Profess can mean to claim something, often falsely: «She professes to know everything.»
fact: «Profess ignorance» means to openly say you do not know something.
fact: Profess can also mean to openly follow a religion.
exercise: «Ben professed his love for football.» What does professed mean here? | Played it very badly | Hid his love for it | *Openly said he loved it | Forgot all about it
exercise: «Lily professes to be a brilliant cook, but her cakes always burn.» Does the sentence suggest that Lily's claim is true? | Yes, she said it, so it must be true | Not sure, because cakes are hard to make | *No, the burnt cakes suggest the claim may not be true | Yes, because burnt cakes are very tasty
exercise: What does «profess ignorance» mean? | To shout loudly in class | *To openly say you do not know something | To know everything about a subject | To run away from a question
test: «The old man professed to be a wizard, yet he could not make even a small spark.» What does the sentence suggest about the old man? | He hid from everyone in the town | *He claimed to be a wizard, but the evidence does not match | He is a famous cook | He is definitely a real wizard
test: Which word means nearly the same as profess, in the sense of openly saying you believe something? | *Declare | Whisper | Forget | Hide
exam: «Maya professed to have read every book in the library, but she had never opened one.» What does the sentence suggest about Maya? | She was too tired to read | She loves reading more than anyone | *Her claim was false | She is the librarian
